Over the weekend I finally did my Y2K testing on my AlphaStation 1000A.  I'm
posting my experiences in case they might be of use to anyone.

Even though all the relevant software, except our own suite, was already
vendor-certified, I had to go through the monkey-motions of testing because
of foolish beauracratic requirements.  So I had to watch the system roll
over through a series of twelve dates ranging from 09-09-1999 to 12-31-2027
(I don't know why), once with power on and once with power off.  And I had
to do it on my production system.  Anyone who'd like to complain about how
foolish this is, is welcome to find the assistant CIO who demanded it, and
the legislature and the auditor's office that pushed him into it, and tell
them.

My system is on a 2.1G HD and a 4.3G HD, and we recently bought and added a
9G HD that we haven't started using, so I made a complete copy of my entire
file system on it (plus a swap partition), then on the Big Day, shut down
the system, changed the bootdef_dev to point to the new hard drive,
unplugged the "real" hard drives, and booted.  I had a ton of trouble
getting the boot to happen; though I couldn't see anything in SRM that was
referring to the old hard drives, it seemed the system wanted to find them,
and I got streams and streams of disk errors on each boot.  Plus swap space
wouldn't load, and so it went into "lazy" mode.  One of these things caused
the system to keep pausing every so often.  But it booted and worked.  As I
don't know that much about that part of the system, I just went with it --
it was adequate to the task.

There were, of course, unexpected wrinkles, but no more than I expected.
<grin>  At one point I forgot to disable some timed jobs in cron and
QMaster, and one went off when I rolled the date forward.  But otherwise
things went pretty smooth.  I rolled the date to just before midnight, then
watched it roll over, for each date.  Then rolled it back to before midnight
again and shut down.  On the crucial New Year's Eve rollover, I had bunches
of jobs going -- Blast running polling a cash register, processing the files
in polling, etc.  Everything ticked over smooth, as expected (but now I can
tell the assistant CIO that without perjuring myself).

By the time I got to 2000-02-29 I was tired of waiting through these long,
error-filled reboots, some of which, due to all those hard drive errors I
referred to, were taking a half hour, so I just did the normal rollovers.
Plus, I had to stay booted because the test key QMaster gave me didn't work,
so I couldn't test QMaster past next July (when my real key expires) except
by keeping the system up the whole time.  So I went all the way to 2027 (by
the way, things are nice then).  Then decided to roll back to 2000-03-01 to
do a power-off rollover test.

Typed the date command, hit Enter, and the CDE screen went white.  The mouse
froze a moment later.  Had to manually power off and back on, then went
through a half-hour-long boot.  By the end of that tension-filled boot, I
had decided not to do the rollover after all (it was almost 6pm by now, I'd
been here eight hours on a Saturday) and just get the system back up.  But I
had to get the system clock to the present before I put the production hard
drives back in, and I had to be absolutely sure that's where it was, so I
wouldn't corrupt those disks.  So when it finally came up on 2000-03-01 I
just set the date back to 1999-07-10.

White screen, mouse freeze.  I wonder what program doesn't like big
backwards date changes?  Changing a few hours works okay, I'd done it a few
times, but changing over months is very, very fatal.  A mystery for another
lifetime, I suppose.  I waited through another painfully long and tortuous
reboot so I could be sure, then shut down, swapped drives, changed
bootdef_dev, and was quite pleased to see my system come back up.  Total
running time, 6.5 hours or so.

Net result: everything's just like the vendors said it was.  *sigh*  And I
know the system can handle even the midnight rollover, so I can make other
plans for this New Year's Eve.  Like sleeping.

