I have two comments.

"Frank J. Perricone" wrote:
> 
> 9G HD that we haven't started using, so I made a complete copy of my entire
> file system on it (plus a swap partition), then on the Big Day, shut down
> the system, changed the bootdef_dev to point to the new hard drive,
> unplugged the "real" hard drives, and booted.  I had a ton of trouble
> getting the boot to happen; though I couldn't see anything in SRM that was
> referring to the old hard drives, it seemed the system wanted to find them,
> and I got streams and streams of disk errors on each boot.  Plus swap space
> wouldn't load, and so it went into "lazy" mode.  One of these things caused
> the system to keep pausing every so often.  But it booted and worked.  As I
> don't know that much about that part of the system, I just went with it --
> it was adequate to the task.

when you copied your system disk, did you change:

	/etc/fstab, so that the partitions pointed to the new disk?
	/etc/rc.config (PAGEFILE, PARTITION, PARTITIONTYPE)
	/sbin/swapdefault (link to primary swap device)
	/etc/fdmns/{root,usr,var}_domain, (if using AdvFS)

If you forgot any of these, it might explain your problems.

> 
> There were, of course, unexpected wrinkles, but no more than I expected.
> <grin>  At one point I forgot to disable some timed jobs in cron and
> QMaster, and one went off when I rolled the date forward.  But otherwise
> things went pretty smooth.  I rolled the date to just before midnight, then
> watched it roll over, for each date.  Then rolled it back to before midnight

There is absolutely no guarantee that a system will function properly if
you set the date backwards.  Doing so pretty much invalidates any tests
that come after that.  Of course, if the tests worked anyhow, you can probably
assume things will work.  If successive tests fail, however, you don't
know if they failed because of some Y2K bug, or just because the date was
set backwards.

To properly execute such tests, you probably need to restore the system
each time after setting the date backwards.

Rob Urban



